{
  "dom_id": "step-e6620",
  "name": "Example: Calculating the Period of a Simple Harmonic Oscillation",
  "problem": "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$$\ny'' + 100y = 0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the oscillation?",
  "worked_solution": "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nwhere the period $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\omega}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 100\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\omega = 10,\n$$\n\nso the period (in seconds) is\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{10} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{5}\\,\\textrm{s}.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
